FAMOUS_ICONS.push(
  { key:'zvenigorod', kind:'christ', title:'The Savior of Zvenigorod (Rublev)', date:'c. 1410s', place:'Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow', medium:'Tempera on wood',
    text:'In 1918 an art restorer found three badly damaged icon panels being used as boards in a woodshed near Zvenigorod, outside Moscow. They had come from a Deësis tier painted by Andrei Rublev, and one of them was this image of Christ.\n\nMost of the paint is gone, but the face survives: calm, gentle, and full of compassion. For many, it is the most moving face of Christ in all of Russian art.',
    look:['The quiet, merciful gaze, very different from the stern Byzantine Pantocrator.','The bare wood around the face, showing how much was lost.','The soft, glowing color typical of Rublev.'] },
  { key:'holyface', kind:'christ', title:'The Holy Face (Ushakov)', date:'1674', place:'Novgorod Museum, Russia', medium:'Tempera on wood',
    text:'Simon Ushakov, the leading painter of the Tsar’s workshops in Moscow, painted several versions of the "Savior Not Made by Hands," the face of Christ on a cloth, following the tradition of the Image of Edessa.\n\nUshakov combined the ancient icon pattern with a softer, more natural modeling learned from Western art. His icons were controversial in his own day, but they became some of the best loved images of Christ in Russia.',
    look:['The cloth held up at the corners, as if by unseen hands.','The face painted with gentle shading, almost like a portrait.','The cross in the halo, the sign of Christ.'] },
  { key:'daphni', kind:'christ', title:'Christ Pantocrator of Daphni', date:'c. 1090–1100', place:'Daphni Monastery, near Athens, Greece', medium:'Mosaic in the dome',
    text:'Looking down from the center of the dome of the church of Daphni, this Christ is one of the most powerful images of the Byzantine golden age. In Byzantine churches the dome stands for heaven, and Christ rules from it over the whole church below.\n\nHis gaze is stern and searching, and his eyes seem to follow the viewer. He holds a richly bound Gospel and blesses the people gathered beneath him.',
    look:['The intense, almost severe expression.','The way the figure bends with the curve of the dome.','The letters IC XC on either side of his head.'] },
  { key:'shepherd', kind:'christ', title:'The Good Shepherd of Ravenna', date:'first half of the 5th century', place:'Mausoleum of Galla Placidia, Ravenna, Italy', medium:'Mosaic',
    text:'Before the rules of icon painting were fixed, early Christians loved to picture Christ as the Good Shepherd who lays down his life for his sheep. In this mosaic over the entrance of a small chapel in Ravenna, Christ sits among his flock in a green landscape, reaching out to feed one of the sheep.\n\nHe is young and beardless, as in much early Christian art, but already royal: he wears gold and purple and holds a tall cross instead of a shepherd’s staff.',
    look:['The cross held like a shepherd’s crook.','The gold and purple robe, the colors of an emperor.','Each sheep turned toward the Shepherd.'] },
  { key:'don', kind:'mary', title:'Theotokos of the Don', date:'late 14th century, attributed to Theophanes the Greek', place:'Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow', medium:'Tempera on wood',
    text:'According to tradition, Grand Prince Dmitry Donskoy carried this icon at the Battle of Kulikovo in 1380, where the Russian forces defeated the Golden Horde, though the icon itself may have been painted a little later. It is attributed to the Byzantine master Theophanes the Greek.\n\nLike the Theotokos of Vladimir, it shows Mary and the Child cheek to cheek in tenderness. On the back of the panel is an icon of the Dormition, the falling asleep of the Mother of God.',
    look:['The Child’s hand reaching up to his mother’s face.','The deep red and gold of the Child’s robe.','Mary’s sorrowful, inward gaze.'] },
  { key:'tikhvin', kind:'mary', title:'Theotokos of Tikhvin', date:'tradition from 1383', place:'Tikhvin Monastery, Russia', medium:'Tempera on wood, in a silver cover',
    text:'According to tradition, this Hodegetria icon appeared in the air above the waters near Tikhvin in 1383, and a church and monastery were built where it came to rest. It became one of the most honored icons of northern Russia.\n\nDuring the Second World War it was taken from Tikhvin and eventually brought to the United States by an Orthodox bishop, who kept it for decades in Chicago, asking that it be returned only when Russia was free. In 2004 his adopted son brought it home to Tikhvin.',
    look:['Mary holding the Child on her arm and pointing to him, the Hodegetria pattern.','The Child’s crossed legs, with the sole of one foot showing.','The jeweled silver cover (riza) that leaves only the faces and hands visible.'] },
  { key:'iveron', kind:'mary', title:'Panagia Portaitissa (Iveron)', date:'tradition from the iconoclast era', place:'Iviron Monastery, Mount Athos, Greece', medium:'Tempera on wood, in a silver cover',
    text:'According to tradition, during the destruction of icons in the ninth century a widow of Nicaea set this icon afloat on the sea rather than see it destroyed. Many years later it appeared off the coast of Mount Athos standing in a pillar of fire, and a holy monk named Gabriel walked across the water to receive it.\n\nThe monks placed it over the gate of their monastery, and it is called Portaitissa, "the Keeper of the Gate." A mark on the Virgin’s chin recalls the story that a soldier once struck the icon and blood flowed from the wound.',
    look:['The wound on Mary’s chin.','The Child held on her left arm, blessing.','The silver-gilt cover adorned by centuries of pilgrims.'] },
  { key:'pochaiv', kind:'mary', title:'Theotokos of Pochaiv', date:'16th century', place:'Pochaiv Lavra, Ternopil region, Ukraine', medium:'Tempera on wood, in a jeweled frame',
    text:'A Greek metropolitan gave this small icon in 1559 to a noblewoman, Anna Hoyska, who gave it to the monks of Pochaiv in 1597. In 1675 it was credited with saving the monastery when the Virgin was seen over the church during a Turkish siege.\n\nFrom 1713 to 1831 Pochaiv was a Greek Catholic monastery of the Basilian order, and in 1773 the icon was solemnly crowned with crowns sent by Pope Clement XIV. Today the Lavra belongs to the Orthodox, and the icon is honored by Ukrainians of both Churches.',
    look:['Mary and the Child cheek to cheek, in the Eleousa pattern.','Small figures of saints painted in the border.','The jeweled frame and crowns of later centuries.'] },
  { key:'philermos', kind:'mary', title:'Our Lady of Philermos', date:'tradition attributes it to St. Luke; venerated since the Middle Ages', place:'National Museum of Montenegro, Cetinje', medium:'Tempera on wood, in a gold and jeweled cover',
    text:'This icon was the patroness of the Knights of St. John, the Knights Hospitaller, who venerated it on the island of Rhodes and carried it with them to Malta in 1523 after Rhodes fell to the Ottomans.\n\nWhen Napoleon took Malta in 1798, the icon was brought to Tsar Paul I of Russia, and after the Russian Revolution it went to the royal family of Serbia. Long thought lost, it was found again in Montenegro, where it is kept as a national treasure. The Sovereign Military Order of Malta still honors Our Lady of Philermos as its patroness.',
    look:['The darkened face of the Virgin, all that can be seen beneath the cover.','The rich gold cover and star-shaped halo given by the Knights.'] },
  { key:'apse', kind:'mary', title:'Theotokos of the Hagia Sophia Apse', date:'867', place:'Hagia Sophia, Istanbul (Constantinople)', medium:'Mosaic in the apse',
    text:'When the veneration of icons was restored after more than a century of iconoclasm, this mosaic of the Mother of God enthroned with the Christ Child was unveiled in the apse of the great church of Constantinople. Patriarch Photius preached a famous sermon at its dedication in 867.\n\nIt was the first great image placed in Hagia Sophia after the iconoclast period, a visible sign that the faith in the Incarnation, and the right to depict it, had triumphed.',
    look:['Mary seated on a jeweled throne without a back, on a cushion.','The Child seated on her lap, facing forward, as on a throne.','The vast gold background of the apse around the small figures.'] },
  { key:'sinaivirgin', kind:'mary', title:'Virgin and Child with Saints Theodore and George', date:'6th century', place:'St. Catherine’s Monastery, Mount Sinai', medium:'Encaustic on wood',
    text:'One of the very few icons to survive from before the iconoclast period, this image shows the Mother of God enthroned with the Child, guarded by two soldier saints, Theodore and George. Behind her, two angels look up to the hand of God reaching down from heaven.\n\nThe painter used three styles at once: the saints are solemn and still, Mary is calm and gentle, and the angels are painted lightly and freely, as beings of another world.',
    look:['The hand of God and the beam of light at the top.','The two soldier saints in their court cloaks, holding crosses.','Mary’s eyes turned away from the viewer, toward something beyond.'] },
  { key:'ustyug', kind:'feast', title:'The Annunciation of Ustyug', date:'c. 1130s', place:'Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow', medium:'Tempera on wood',
    text:'One of the oldest surviving Russian icons, painted in Novgorod. The Archangel Gabriel greets the Virgin, who stands in quiet acceptance, holding the purple thread she was spinning for the Temple veil, according to an ancient tradition.\n\nUnusually, the icon shows the Christ Child already present within her as she says yes to God, and at the top Christ appears as the Ancient of Days, sending forth the Holy Spirit.',
    look:['The small figure of the Christ Child on Mary’s breast.','The ray of grace descending from heaven.','Gabriel’s graceful step and raised hand of greeting.'] },
  { key:'george', kind:'saints', title:'The Miracle of St. George and the Dragon', date:'15th century, Novgorod school', place:'Russian Museum, St. Petersburg', medium:'Tempera on wood',
    text:'In the bright red icons of Novgorod, St. George rides a white horse and drives his lance into the dragon’s mouth, while the hand of God blesses him from above. The story of the dragon is a medieval legend, but the image speaks of the victory of faith over evil, won by Christ in his martyrs.\n\nSt. George was one of the most beloved saints of Rus’, and Novgorod painters made his image a masterpiece of movement and color.',
    look:['The brilliant red background typical of Novgorod.','The white horse leaping almost off the panel.','The hand of God blessing the saint from the corner.'] },
  { key:'michael', kind:'saints', title:'The Archangel Michael', date:'14th century', place:'Byzantine and Christian Museum, Athens', medium:'Tempera and gold on wood',
    text:'Painted in an aristocratic workshop of Constantinople, this icon shows the Archangel Michael, commander of the heavenly hosts, as a court official of the emperor of heaven.\n\nHe holds a messenger’s staff and a transparent sphere marked with the monogram or cross of Christ, a sign that the angels serve God’s rule over all creation. His soft, thoughtful face is typical of the late Byzantine Palaiologan style.',
    look:['The orb held in his left hand.','The great wings folded behind him, painted in gold.','The rich imperial garments of a heavenly courtier.'] },
  { key:'peter', kind:'saints', title:'St. Peter of Sinai', date:'6th–7th century', place:'St. Catherine’s Monastery, Mount Sinai', medium:'Encaustic on wood',
    text:'This large early icon shows St. Peter with the keys of the Kingdom and a cross on a long staff, the sign of his martyrdom. It is one of the earliest icons of the apostle, and one of the finest works in the hot-wax technique of late antiquity.\n\nAbove him are three medallions, probably of Christ in the center, with the Mother of God and St. John the Evangelist on either side.',
    look:['The keys in Peter’s hand.','The lifelike, rugged face and white hair.','The three medallions above his head.'] }
);
